Date: Fri, 14 Jan 1994 18:13:17 -0800
From: vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner)
Subject: BOT: How do trees drink

How do trees drink?

Sap lifting in tall trees is not caused by suction, since, as has been
pointed out, a vacuum can only support a column of water about 30 feet
high, while many trees grow to 10 times that height.  

The current theory involves water movement along a potential
(concentration) gradient.  In well hydrated soil, the water potential
is about 0 bars, while in partially humid air it may be minus several
hundred bars.  A process called transpiration provides the energy for
bulk material flow along this potential gradient.  As water evaporates
from the surface of leaves, part of the state-change energy is used to
pull water from neighboring cells into the mesophyll of the leaf.
This change in potential in turn pulls water from xylem (one of the
vascular tissues of a plant) further down the stem, and so on, all the
way to the roots.

This process requires no energy input from the plant.  In fact, either
the leaves of the plant can be replaced by another suitable
evaporating surface, or the vascular tissue can be replaced by a clean
glass tube, with the same effect.  The actual physical mechanism is
probably capillary action, which involves the weight of the water
column being supported by the adhesion of individual water molecules to
the interior cavity walls of xylem cells.  

The interior of such cavities is maintained at less than atmospheric
pressure (in fact, some insects get "stuck" when they try to drink
sap).  In water which is fairly free of dissolved gasses (which are
filtered out in the roots), tensile strengths of several hundred to
perhaps several thousand bars can be supported.  Large cuts in the
vasculature, or bubbles in the water column (formed during freezing in
winter) will cause the column to fall, but otherwise it is fairly
self-sustaining. 

--Vince

