relationship would be damaging and limiting to them.

>I think that alternatives to monogamy/polygamy must also be included
>in this definition.  For example, relationships which are not based on
>sex, such as those between family members, coworkers, or members of an
>organization (see below) may also be Extropian.

I decided to focus on love/sex relationships as a subset of all
relationships, for the topic of my VirtSem, but feel free to explore. I'm
interested in these too.

>Rather than using a
>rejection of the cultural norm as a basis, I might suggest an
>embracing of the Extropian Principles (v2.5), either wholely or in
>part, as a criterion.

Yes, good idea. I've thought along these lines before, but for some reason
did't include this in my original essay.

I would add that a good extropian relationship should not force either
(any) party to turn away from the principles. (See my argument against the
Morman multiple wives above.)

Let's have a look:
1. Boundless Expansion - This primarily were I get the rejection of
"cultural limitations and expectations".

2. Self-Transformation - A relationship should be supportive and flexible
to the changing of the participants. The relationship should be open to
change in its terms, based on the changing needs and desires of its
participants.

3. Dynamic Optimism - Going for the most you can get while keeping in mind
the limitations and desires of the individuals involved. This has a lot to
do with what I called "rational self-determination". The term I used did
not, however, stress "Going for the most you can get," which I believe *is*
important.

4. Intelligent Technology - Fun, but I think I'll leave this to your own
imagination. (I could perhaps refer you to bOING! bOING! a few issues back,
the Sex Candy for Happy Mutants issue, and Future Sex magazine for some
references.) Virtual Love and Tiny Sex could also be evaluated under the
principle.

5. Spontaneous Order - Back to rejecting "cultural limitations and
expectations". No rules but the ones you make between each other. 

Good stuff here, this'll definately go into the final essay.
