Edgar Swank's says:

> I was shocked to read Mike Price spouting this complete nonsense
>  [about trees using negative pressures to suck up water]
> Mike may understand advanced physics but has apparently forgotten what
> he should have learned in high school. [...] Of course there is no
> such thing as "negative pressure".  

I can understand Edgar Swank's consternation - my reaction too when
someone/Encyclopedia Britannica invoked negative pressures to explain
sap transport.  Perhaps Edgar would have been happier if I'd used the
term tension?

Pressure is force across an area.  Force is a vector (ie magnitude and
_direction_).  Reverse the direction of the force and the pressure
changes sign.  Eg place a metal rod between a vice and squeeze.  The rod
is under compression (positive pressure).  Fix the rod at the top and
attach a weight to the bottom and it's under tension (negative
pressure).  Negative pressures /tensions are adhesion or bonding causing
extension.

> Of course there is no such thing as "negative pressure".  Even Linda
> Lovelace can't suck better than a perfect vacuum

What if her mouth was filled with contact adhesive?  Something that
bonded with skin.... 

> [..] A vacuum can't pull anything.

Agreed, but other molecules can.  For instance if the leaves at the top
of the tree are extracting the sap from the xylem with some active
transport mechanism.

> I'm not a botanist, so I don't know the details of how trees work.

Me neither!  :-)

> But capillary action in the very narrow xylem tubes could account 
> for it.

I doubt it.  Capillary action works in contact with air.  If the xylem
is damaged (as by the tunnelling of the Dutch Elm beetle) and air enters
then the xylem tube fails (according to Britannica).  Therefore the
transport mechanism is not capillary action.  As Sherlock Holmes says,
once you've eliminated the impossible....

