X-People,

I saw this on sci.space.news and sci.space.policy (the new space
politics group). I'll trust all of you know what DC-X is. During the
past two years, there have been NSS "phone tree" alerts and net pleas
for people to call their congressman to express support for the program.

Late last year, bureaucratic foot-dragging and technical problems on
the part of the team itself (and, it seems obvious now, political pressure
from the PTB that like the competition a lot more) caused the DC-X to
cease flight testing before all goals had been reached. Of course, everyone
in relevant districts was urged to call their congresscritter, get more
funding, and they got about $ 50 million to continue the testing and 
design the DC-X follow-on vehicle. The testing was expected to cost $ 5 
million or so, if the vehicle didn't spend too much time in storage...

(At the same time, the price was the program was transferred from BMDO
to ARPA. ARPA however doesn't seem to want to fly it.)

Well, Henry Vandebilt tells the facts a lot better than I can. X-folks
thinking about going to the government trough had better look at this
chain of events very closely.

